    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d271">

                <pb facs="0644" n="602" sameAs="frus:frus1951v04p1/pg602"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 271</seg>
                </opener>
                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.00/4–2351: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy (Dunn) to the Secretary of
                            State
                    </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d271-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-04-23T20:00:00+01:00">April 23, 1951—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4803. This spring’s municipal-provincial election campaign which is now underway
                    and which will culminate May 27 to June 10 with elections in 60 of Italy’s 95
                    provinces, will involve over two-thirds Ital electorate. (Elections in fall will
                    be relatively anti-climax involving for most part south and islands.) The
                    campaign will be fought not on local issues but on basic issues of Ital
                    participation in Western alignment and of democracy versus Commie
                    totalitarianism.</p>
                <p>In view above, it wld be helpful here if Dept and other US agencies keep campaign
                    in mind when considering questions of importance to Italy during coming weeks.
                    Dept will, of course, be aware desirability of lending maximum indirect
                    assistance to democratic parties for example by postponing wherever possible
                    announcement of actions unfavorable to Italy until after June 10 or by
                    announcing favorable actions at strategic moments during campaign.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d271-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dunn</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d271-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d271-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to Paris for MacArthur and
                        OSR and to London.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d271-f2" n="2" xml:id="d271-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 4776 to Rome, April 24, the Department of State concurred with these
                    recommendations. (765.00/4–2451)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
